  "body": "Currently the specs [says](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#prompt-to-unload-a-document)\r\n\r\n> The user agent is encouraged to avoid asking the user for confirmation if it judges that doing so would be annoying, deceptive, or pointless. A simple heuristic might be that if the user has not interacted with the document, the user agent would not ask for confirmation before unloading it.\r\n\r\nSince this text was written, the concept of [sticky activation](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#sticky-activation) was introduced. Furthermore, I've been informed that Chrome never asks for confirmation on windows without sticky activation.\r\n\r\nAre other browsers interested in tightening up the language here, now that we have the \"sticky activation\" concept? In particular, we'd change\r\n\r\n> If document's active sandboxing flag set does not have its sandboxed modals flag set, and the returnValue attribute of the event object is not the empty string, or if the event was canceled, then the user agent may ask the user to confirm that they wish to unload the document.\r\n\r\nto something like\r\n\r\n> If all of the following are true:\r\n>\r\n> - document's active sandboxing flag set does not have its sandboxed modals flag set;\r\n> - document's relevant global object has sticky activation; and\r\n> - the returnValue attribute of the event object is not the empty string, or the event was canceled\r\n>\r\n> then the user agent may ask the user to confirm that they wish to unload the document.\r\n\r\ni.e. we'd move sticky activation from \"something a browser might consider to avoid annoyance\" to a must-level prerequisite.\r\n\r\n/cc @annevk @cdumez",
